# Valgrind suppressions for tests/ctgrind.rs — run via `verify.sh --ctgrind`.
#
# This file is a record of what has been examined and permitted, not a silencer.
# It contains exactly two entries, both about *this crate*.
#
# # Why there is no "startup noise" group any more
#
# Earlier revisions enumerated the libtest, Rust-std and glibc startup reports
# (`free` and its inlined allocator, `__strlen_avx2`, `_int_realloc`,
# `__memchr_avx2`, `_dl_non_dynamic_init`, `pthread_getattr_np`, getopts, and the
# `setcc`-free libtest paths) with the exact frames each produced. Those frames
# are internal to three projects that all move independently, so the file kept
# breaking: entries generated against valgrind 3.24 stopped matching on 3.22,
# 3.26 and 3.27, and the Rust-std frames seen on a GitHub runner (thread-local
# lazy init, the mpmc channel, stack-overflow bookkeeping) appear nowhere in a
# local build at all. Every one of those failures was announced as "memcheck
# reported an unsuppressed secret-dependent branch", which is a finding in this
# crate -- and none of them were.
#
# tools/ctgrind.sh now classifies instead: a report whose stack contains a
# `xchacha20_blake3_siv` frame fails the run, and anything else is reported as
# what it is -- startup and teardown noise from other people's code -- with a
# count so it is visible rather than silent. That is version-proof by
# construction, and it is strictly stronger for this crate: a secret-dependent
# branch *here* is caught wherever it is, including in code inlined into a test
# body, because the frame names come from DWARF.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The SIV accept/reject decision — the only entries in this file
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# These two ARE in this crate and are permitted deliberately.
#
# `decrypt` and `decrypt_in_place_detached` end in
#     if bool::from(auth_ok) { Ok(..) } else { Err(..) }
# where `auth_ok` comes from the tag, which comes from the secret key. That
# branch therefore depends on secret-derived data, and it is unavoidable: SIV
# needs the plaintext before it can compute the tag, so decrypt-then-verify is
# inherent to the mode (the crate documents this). The decision reveals exactly
# one bit that is already public — whether this ciphertext authenticates.
#
# This must NOT be read as "the tag comparison is unchecked". The comparison
# *feeding* the branch is verified separately and mechanically:
# `constant_time_eq_does_not_branch_on_operands` in tests/ctgrind.rs poisons both
# operands, requires valgrind to report nothing from the comparison itself, and
# only unpoisons the resulting `Choice` before branching on it. The timing screen
# in tests/security.rs additionally checks position-independence end to end.
#
# Matching on the mangled name means a *new* function in this crate that branched
# on secret data would not be suppressed and would fail the run.

{
   siv-decrypt-accept-reject-decision
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:_RNvCs*_20xchacha20_blake3_siv7decrypt
}

{
   siv-decrypt-in-place-accept-reject-decision
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:_RNvCs*_20xchacha20_blake3_siv25decrypt_in_place_detached
}